thread:
maya: Heads-up — ObjC client hitting partial-JSON crashes while streaming SSE from /v1/responses. Repro on iOS 14 simulator. Happens after ~8–12KB chunks, error: 'Unexpected EOF while parsing object'. Anyone seen UTF-8/fragmentation issues with NSURLSession dataTask?

oscar: Yep. Foundation's NSJSONSerialization isn't incremental — if you feed an incomplete chunk you'll get parsing errors. Are you trying to parse as soon as you append raw bytes?

maya: Exactly. My current handler is basically: 
```
- (void)urlSession:(NSURLSession *)session dataTask:(NSURLSessionDataTask *)dataTask didReceiveData:(NSData *)data {
  [self.buffer appendData:data];
  NSError *err = nil;
  id obj = [NSJSONSerialization JSONObjectWithData:self.buffer options:0 error:&err];
  if (obj) { [self handleObject:obj]; [self.buffer setLength:0]; }
}
```

reza: Two separate things biting you: (1) SSE framing (events separated by double newline) and (2) UTF-8 multi-byte sequences split across TCP packets. If you try to parse raw buffer as JSON you'll hit either partial JSON or invalid UTF-8.

oscar: For SSE specifically, the stream usually emits events like: `data: {...}

`. Instead of trying to parse arbitrary buffer lengths, split incoming bytes on the SSE event delimiter (`

`) and parse each event's `data:` payload individually. That avoids most mid-object truncation.

maya: Good call. The server is sending NDJSON-ish SSE, so splitting on `

` might work. But what about multi-byte UTF-8 split inside a single SSE `data:` chunk? `NSString initWithData:encoding:` can return nil if the bytes end mid-codepoint.

sam: Two options: (A) operate on bytes and only convert to NSString after you verify you have a complete UTF-8 sequence, or (B) use an incremental UTF-8 decoder. A simple pragmatic hack: when you find an event boundary, try to make an NSString; if it fails, keep appending until it succeeds (usually just one more packet). In practice most splits are short.

reza: If you want robust approach, implement a tiny state machine that scans bytes and tracks brace depth for JSON objects while respecting string escapes. Incrementally count `{`/`}` bytes but ignore braces inside strings (track `"` and `\` escapes). When depth returns to 0 and you're not inside a string, you have a complete JSON object. Works even if the SSE server embeds one big JSON per event.

oscar: Example pseudo: 
```
// append data bytes to buffer
int depth = 0; bool inString = false; bool escape = false;
for (byte b : buffer) {
  if (escape) { escape = false; continue; }
  if (b == '\') { escape = true; continue; }
  if (b == '"') { inString = !inString; continue; }
  if (!inString) { if (b == '{') depth++; if (b == '}') depth--; }
  if (depth == 0 && !inString) { // complete object boundary found }
}
```

oscar: Also watch out for backpressure. If the client appends faster than you can parse, the buffer will grow and memory will spike. NSURLSessionDataTask supports `suspend`/`resume` so you can throttle network ingestion: when buffer > X KB, call `[dataTask suspend]`, process until buffer < Y KB, then `[dataTask resume]`. Works but be careful with timers/timeouts.

maya: Nice. I'd been considering letting the runloop catch up but suspending seems cleaner. Any gotchas for auth? We see 401 mid-stream sometimes when refresh token happens. The server kills the connection, so we reconnect and lose state.

auth-bot: Tip: if an auth refresh can happen mid-stream, include a short retry policy: on 401, attempt token refresh once, then reconnect with exponential backoff + jitter. If your protocol supports it, send a `Last-Event-ID` or conversation cursor to resume; if not, reconcile at application level (re-request last N tokens).

reza: Agree. For Redwood flows we recommend ephemeral access tokens that are valid for the session lifetime; if you must refresh, try to do a graceful reconnect and avoid automatic retry storms (use backoff). Also surface a metric for 'stream_resume' so ops can alert when clients are constantly reconnecting.

oscar: One more edge: if you suspend the task to apply backpressure, make sure to periodically call `resume` even if parsing is slow, otherwise server keepalives may time out. Alternatively, implement a small queue so you resume briefly just to drain a packet.

maya: Actionable plan for me: 1) switch to splitting on SSE `

` boundaries, 2) implement simple brace-depth check per event, 3) add suspend/resume threshold (buffer > 256KB suspend, resume at < 64KB), 4) handle 401 with single refresh + backoff. I'll try this and post logs.

